From 542bce5516e5687441f1203425d6e31d2b703298 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: haowen1993 <39072333+haowen1993@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 13:17:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] perf(scripts): resolve derived-locale art availability
 from the index API
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The availability sidecar asked the CDN once per printing (`GET` + `Range:
bytes=0-0`). That was right about SCRYFALL — no Scryfall field records whether a
derived locale has art for an English printing id — and wrong about the CDN
operator: 大学院废墟 publishes its own index, and for every printing it answers
exactly this question (`zhs_image_url` is the URL when the art exists, null when
it does not). Measured on Doctor Who, the index and a full probe agree on all 1178
printings — 398 available, zero disagreements in either direction — so the probe
was buying the same boolean for ~70,000 requests instead of ~70.

Full pool, measured: 336 sets, 71,788 printings, 346 requests, 355 s, 40,887 ids
written. The old path at the same scale is ~68,000 requests (hours).

The probe is KEPT, not replaced: it depends on nothing but the CDN, so it remains
the fallback when the index is unreachable or has not caught up. Both writers emit
the same document and the same `ids` list — only `algorithm` and `source` record
which produced a file, so the client cannot tell them apart. Select with
PHASE_AVAILABILITY_METHOD=index|probe; a typo is rejected rather than silently
falling back, because the wrong choice costs an hour.

Two API parameters are load-bearing and documented at the call site:

  * `include_extras=true` — the index excludes supplemental cards by default, and
    Doctor Who's 40 planechase cards are extras. Without it the WHO answer is 358
    printings instead of 398, silently dropping art the client would prefer.
  * `unique=scryfall_id` — the default deduplicates to one representative version
    per Oracle object (318 for the same set), which cannot answer a per-printing
    question at all.

Rate limiting is by request FREQUENCY, not concurrency: a burst draws 429s and the
response carries no Retry-After or rate-limit header, so pacing is ours (3 workers
plus a shared inter-request delay, and multiplicative backoff with jitter on 429).
The coverage check is what makes that safe — it refuses to write a sidecar when any
expected printing was never returned, so an interrupted run fails loudly instead of
recording "no art" for printings it never asked about. That check caught a real
21,510-printing shortfall during development.
